# Scenario 5 — L-shaped block with over-the-block teg_mode
#
# Geometry:
#   L-block: tall arm (0,0)-(100,400)  +  wide base (0,0)-(400,100)
#   Notch region: x=100–400, y=100–400
#   src block:    (500,150)-(600,250)
#
# What to look for in the topology explorer:
#   • All V-trunk candidates land at Hanan-grid x values: 0, 100, 400, 500, 600
#     and at midpoints: x=50, x=250, x=450, x=550.
#     x=300 is NOT a Hanan-grid coordinate — that is why the BDD test
#     "TRUNK_V@x300 has a bridge segment" is xfail.
#   • For any V trunk (e.g. TRUNK_V@x250), x=250 is inside the wide base's
#     x range [0,400], so block L is connected "Direct" — no H stub needed
#     and no bridge is generated (trunk_inside_any = true in C++ logic).
#   • The OVER mode bridge would only fire when the trunk falls in an
#     axis-aligned GAP between rects in X.  The L-shape's notch is a
#     diagonal notch, not a simple X-direction gap, so the OVER logic
#     never triggers.
#
# To extend the implementation to handle L-shapes, you would need to detect
# cases where the trunk is inside one rect but NOT inside another rect of
# the same block, and generate a bridge to connect the unreachable rect.

add_block src 500 150 600 250
add_block L rect 0 0 100 400 rect 0 0 400 100 teg_mode over

def_layer 4 M4 H TOP 0
def_layer 5 M5 V TOP 0

add_net clk src.tx L.rx

run_bundler STRICT
generate_topologies_for_bundle clk src L
run_planner
run_nuts

#visualize_topologies clk
visualize

